<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayasimha II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1015–1042</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Jagadekamalla I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayasimha II, also called Jagadekamalla I, was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> from about 1015 to 1042. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> under Rajendra I, who defeated him at Maski around 1020, and the Paramara king Bhoja, against whom he held his northern frontier. His reign saw a flowering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durgasimha">Durgasimha</a>'s Panchatantra.</p></div>
